            <ArticleTitle>Angiopoietin-2, its soluble receptor Tie-2, and metabolic syndrome components in a population-based sample.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2038-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/oby.21632</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Since angiopoietin-2 (Ang-2) levels strongly correlate with cardiovascular mortality and subclinical cardiovascular disease, it was hypothesized that levels of Ang-2 and its soluble receptor (sTie-2) were associated with the metabolic syndrome (MetS) and individual MetS components.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Within the population-based Study of Health in Pomerania, two sets of analyses were performed. First, Ang-2 and sTie-2 were related to the prevalence of MetS and its components cross-sectionally (n = 3,205). Second, the association between baseline Ang-2 and sTie-2 and incident MetS or longitudinal changes in its components in 1,295 individuals was investigated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">High Ang-2 levels (90th percentile), compared with low Ang-2 levels (10th percentile), were positively associated with MetS (OR: 1.78) and with the following MetS criteria: increased triglycerides, lower HDL cholesterol, and higher non-fasting glucose. Furthermore, high sTie-2 levels (90th percentile), compared with low levels (10th percentile), were positively related to MetS (OR: 1.58) and most of its components. However, Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were not associated with incident MetS or longitudinal change in components of MetS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were cross-sectionally associated with MetS and several of its components. However, Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were not associated with incident MetS or changes in individual MetS components during follow-up.</AbstractText>
